{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789397646,"nonce":"XukqgTEoOUNp5MF3+JMtqlIlfYg=","title":"Python: a caught exception is never released","message":"`raise ValueError(\"odd\")` allocates the instance and the box it travels\nin as the pending exception; a handler that catches it clears the\npending slot and leaves both behind. pressure/exceptions.py, raising\nevery other step, grew by about 45 bytes a step (27 MB at 2,000 steps\nto 72 MB at 2,000,000).\n\nFix: the handler releases what it caught (`zb_release_caught`: free the\ninstance, then `zyntax_box_free` the box) when it is known to keep\nnothing of it. `scope::handler_keeps_exception` decides: a bare `raise`\nkeeps it, and with `except E as e` any use of `e` other than a field\nread, a conversion to text (`str`, `repr`, f-string interpolation) or an\nargument to `print`/`len`/`type`/`isinstance`/`bool` keeps it, as does\na method call on it or a nested def/lambda/generator reading it.\n\nA clause without a name releases right after clearing the pending slot.\nA named clause releases wherever it leaves: at the end of its body, and\nbefore every `return`, `break`, `continue`, `raise` or pending escape\nthat leaves the clause (`HandlerCtl`, with a per-clause loop depth so an\nexit from a loop inside the clause releases nothing). A return value is\ncomputed into a temporary before the release.\n\nThe instance's own fields are not released: the message string may be a\nliteral, and a user subclass may hold anything.","files":null}]}